<HelpLayout title="Progression" description="How Badger changes your weights: progression rules, training maxes, percentage sets, waves, stage ladders, the progressive overload suggestion and auto-progress.">
  <h1>Progression</h1>

  <p>This page covers every way Badger changes the weight you lift next: progression rules on a routine, the progressive overload suggestion, and auto-progress. Building the routine itself is on the <a href="/help/routines/">Routines</a> page.</p>

  <hr />

  <h2 id="why">Why did my weight go up, or not?</h2>
  <p>Look at the line under the exercise on the routine log preview. If the exercise has a progression rule, that line names the part of the rule that fired, or says why the weight is holding. No line means no rule, or a rule with nothing to do here; the weight comes from another source in this table.</p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>What sets the weight</th><th>Where you see it</th><th>Where it's set</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><a href="#rules">Progression rule</a></td><td>The routine log preview, as a proposal you can change</td><td>On an exercise in a routine, or built into a program</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="/help/routines/#weight-source">Last-performance prefill</a></td><td>Starting weights copied from your last session</td><td>The routine's weight source</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#progressive-overload">Progressive overload suggestion</a></td><td>A <strong>Suggested</strong> line above the weight field while you log</td><td><a href="/help/settings/#coaching">Settings → Coaching</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#auto-progress">Auto-progress</a></td><td>The workout summary, as an offer to update the routine</td><td><a href="/help/settings/#coaching">Settings → Coaching</a> (off by default)</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="/help/tools/#deload">Deload week</a></td><td>A banner on the Today screen, and lighter weights in the preview</td><td><a href="/help/settings/#coaching">Settings → Coaching → Deload</a></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>How they fit together:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>A rule's proposal replaces the prefilled weight on that exercise's working sets. Exercises without a rule start from the weight source.</li>
    <li>An exercise with a rule gets no overload suggestion, and auto-progress skips it.</li>
    <li>The overload suggestion and auto-progress only ever raise a weight. A rule can also lower one, for example after repeated misses.</li>
    <li>A deload week pauses a rule's count. See <a href="#holds-and-deloads">Holds, misses and deload weeks</a>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="rules">Progression rules</h2>
  <p>A progression rule is a sentence on an exercise in a routine that says what happens to its weight next session, based on the last one. This is the rule StrongLifts uses, as the app shows it:</p>
  <div class="callout">
    <p>After <strong>3</strong> misses, drop <strong>10%</strong>. When all working sets hit the plan, add <strong>2.5 kg</strong>.</p>
  </div>
  <ul>
    <li>The bold parts are chips. Tap one to change its number.</li>
    <li>Badger checks the clauses from top to bottom and uses the first one that matches. If none matches, the weight stays where it is.</li>
    <li>The increment shows in your unit: 2.5 kg if you log in kilos, 5 lbs in pounds, the figures the program itself uses.</li>
    <li>Each exercise in a routine day can have one rule. The rule belongs to that slot: if you tap <strong>Replace exercise</strong> in its <strong>⋮</strong> menu, Badger asks first, because the new exercise starts without the rule (and without its training max).</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Six of the <a href="/help/routines/#programs">built-in programs</a> come with their rules already written.</p>

  <h3 id="writing-a-rule">Write a rule</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Open a routine day and tap an exercise to open its planned sets.</li>
    <li>In the <strong>Progression</strong> section at the top, tap <strong>Linear</strong> or <strong>Double progression</strong>. Badger writes a starting sentence.</li>
    <li>Tap any number in the sentence (the increment, the percentage, the number of misses, the rep threshold) and type a new value.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>The sentence stays exactly as you leave it. To add a reminder for yourself ("only while I'm eating enough"), use <strong>Notes</strong>; the rule runs the same either way. To remove the rule, tap <strong>None</strong> and confirm.</p>
  <p id="assisted-lifts">On an assisted exercise, like an assisted pull-up, the weight is help, so less is better. Linear and Double progression then work the other way: the sentence reads <em>drop 2.5 kg</em> when you hit the plan, and <em>add</em> after repeated misses. GZCLP and 5/3/1 aren't offered there.</p>

  <h3 id="set-up-progression">Add rules to a routine you already have</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>On the routine detail screen, open the <strong>⋮ menu</strong> and tap <strong>Set up progression</strong>.</li>
    <li>Read the list of sentences it would write, day by day.</li>
    <li>Confirm to write them.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>Exercises with a rep range get double progression, and fixed-rep exercises get linear. Exercises that already have a rule, or no weight, are skipped. When the same exercise, with the same preset, appears on two or more newly ruled days, those days are marked <em>Progresses together on every day it appears</em> (see <a href="#progressing-together">Lifts that progress together</a>).</p>

  <h3 id="reading-a-rule">Where a rule shows</h3>
  <p>On the routine detail screen, the rule's sentence sits under the exercise name, in the same words the editor used. On a day where some exercises have rules, the others read <strong>No rule &middot; progression hints active</strong>.</p>

  <h2 id="when-a-rule-acts">What the routine log preview shows</h2>
  <p>A rule proposes a weight on the routine log preview, before you start, and you can change it there. The routine's planned sets stay as they are.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>The proposed weight is in the working sets. The line under them names the part of the rule that fired, for example <em>Rule met: all sets hit the plan last session &middot; tap edit to override</em>.</li>
    <li>On a waved day, or with a training max, the line starts with where the number came from: <em>Week 2 of 4 &middot; from TM 135 lbs &middot; Rule met: the cycle finished &middot; tap edit to override</em>. A stage ladder shows <em>Stage 2 of 3</em> in place of the week.</li>
    <li>When a rule holds the weight, the line says why, for example <em>Holding: the driver set fell short of its rep target</em>. When the rule has nothing to do, there's no line.</li>
    <li>Reps come from the routine's plan. On a laddered program such as StrongLifts or GZCLP, a rule can also move you to the next <a href="#stages">stage</a>, a different set scheme already written into the routine.</li>
    <li>Nothing is recorded until you tap <strong>Start</strong>, so backing out and opening the preview again shows the same proposal.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3 id="override">Use a different weight</h3>
  <p>Tap the <strong>edit</strong> pencil on the exercise and type your weight. The line keeps the reason and adds <em>overridden</em>. The rule stays in place for next time.</p>

  <h3 id="holds-and-deloads">Holds, misses and deload weeks</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Miss the minimum once and a 5/3/1 training max holds for that whole cycle.</strong> 5/3/1 only raises it at a cycle boundary, so a week 1 shortfall still counts three weeks later. The preview says <em>Holding: the plan's minimum was missed this cycle</em>. Setting the week by hand clears it.</li>
    <li><strong>A <a href="/help/tools/#deload">deload week</a> pauses a rule's count without resetting it.</strong> It doesn't advance a cycle, and misses you'd already built up are still there afterward.</li>
    <li>A deload week written into the program, such as 5/3/1's fourth week, is an ordinary week and counts.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="training-max">Training max</h2>
  <p>A training max is a number per lift, set a little below your real one-rep max, that programs such as 5/3/1 and nSuns take their percentages from. It's never loaded on the bar.</p>
  <p>An exercise that uses one shows it under its name on the routine detail screen, for example <strong>Training max 135 lbs &middot; 120 → 130 → 135 ›</strong>.</p>
  <p>Badger asks for your training maxes when you make a routine active. If a lift in it has a rule that needs a training max you haven't set, the <strong>Set your training maxes</strong> sheet opens with one row per lift. It works for any routine with a training max rule, including routines you build and imported <code>.badger</code> files.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>At the top, pick <strong>I know my 1-rep max</strong> or <strong>I know my training max</strong>. With a 1-rep max, Badger stores 90% of the number you type, rounded to your plates. A training max is stored exactly as typed.</li>
    <li>Fill in a row for each lift. A lift you have logged before is filled in for you from your history (90% of your best estimated 1-rep max) and marked <strong>From your history</strong>. Change it if it's off.</li>
    <li>Tap <strong>Save</strong>.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>A row you leave blank stays unset. <strong>Later</strong> closes the sheet without saving anything. Lifts that share one training max share one row; when two rows are the same exercise, the day names under each one tell them apart.</p>
  <p>You can also set one later. Tap the lift's training max line on the routine detail screen, or the <strong>Set a training max to use this rule</strong> line on the routine log preview. On the preview it opens the same sheet for that lift.</p>
  <p>After that, the rule moves it. The same sheet lists every move, marked as coming from the rule, from you, or from the first time it was set. Your own edit always wins. A shared routine file doesn't carry your training max, so whoever opens it sets their own.</p>

  <h2 id="percent-sets">Percentage sets</h2>
  <p>A percentage set is planned as a share of the training max (85%) instead of a weight (100 kg). The routine log preview works out the weight and rounds it to something you can load.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Open the exercise's planned sets in the routine.</li>
    <li>On a set, tap <strong>+ % of max</strong> and enter the percentage.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>The percentage replaces the weight field. Tap <strong>Use a weight instead</strong> and the weight you last typed comes back. The percentage controls appear when the exercise has a rule, when the day is waved, or when the set already uses them.</p>
  <p>On the preview:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>The weight is rounded to your gym's plates, the bar you have pinned, the machine's step size, and whether the machine is logged per side. The <a href="/help/tools/">plate calculator</a> and the warm-up calculator round the same way.</li>
    <li>A percentage that lands below the bar is raised to the bar, and the preview says so.</li>
    <li>Until the exercise has a training max, the preview shows <strong>Set a training max to use this rule</strong>. Tap it to open the <a href="#training-max">Set your training maxes</a> sheet for that lift.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Percentage and plain-weight sets can sit in the same day. The routine's weight source and history scope don't apply to a percentage set.</p>

  <h3 id="percent-of-another-lift">A percentage of another lift</h3>
  <p>To base a variation on the lift it assists, such as close-grip bench at 80% of your bench, pick that lift in the <strong>Percent of</strong> row.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Open the variation's planned sets. Once it has at least one percentage set, a <strong>Percent of</strong> row appears under Progression.</li>
    <li>Change it from <strong>This lift</strong> to the lift the percentage comes from. The list shows the other lifts in this routine.</li>
  </ol>
  <ul>
    <li>The percentages now follow that lift's training max or top set. Change the bench's training max and the close-grip weights follow it the same session.</li>
    <li>The lift you pick needs its own progression rule. Without one it has no number to lend, and the preview says so.</li>
    <li>Remove that lift from the routine and the row reads <strong>Not in this routine</strong>. The percentages go back to the variation's own number.</li>
    <li>This shares the number only. To share the number and the miss count, see <a href="#progressing-together">Lifts that progress together</a>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="driver-set">The driver set</h2>
  <p>The driver is the one set whose logged reps a rule reads, such as the last main set in 5/3/1 or the key set in nSuns. Tap <strong>Drives progression</strong> on that set in the planned-sets editor, and a clause like <em>If the driver set reaches 8, add 5 lbs</em> reads your reps on it.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>When the set has a label such as <strong>5+</strong>, the sentence uses it: <em>If the 5+ set reaches 8, add 5 lbs</em>.</li>
    <li>An exercise has one driver (one per week on a waved day). Marking a new one clears the old. A warmup can't be the driver.</li>
    <li>Driver sets are usually <a href="/help/routines/#amrap-targets">open-topped</a> (5+), so the reps you get tell the rule how strong you were that day. Log the real number: a 5+ taken to 12 and one stopped at 5 lead to different weeks.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="waves">Waved days (a cycle of weeks)</h2>
  <p>A waved day runs a different session each week of a cycle, such as 5/3/1's four-week wave. Each set belongs to a week, and the preview shows only this week's sets.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>On the routine detail screen, open the day's <strong>Day options</strong> and set <strong>Wave</strong> to the number of weeks.</li>
    <li>In each exercise's planned sets, pick the week for every set. Tap <strong>Every week</strong> for a set that belongs in all of them, such as accessory work.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>To turn the wave off, set it below two weeks.</p>

  <h3 id="which-week">Which week the routine is on</h3>
  <p>The whole routine is on one week, shown as <strong>Week 2 of 4</strong> in the <strong>WAVE</strong> section near the top of the routine detail screen. Every waved day in the routine follows it.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>It moves to the next week once you've trained every day of the cycle, in any order. Skipping a day doesn't cost you a week.</li>
    <li>To change it, tap the section and pick a week in <strong>Which week?</strong>. It saves immediately.</li>
    <li>Today shows it too, at the top of your routine days, for example "Week 1 of 4 &middot; 3 of 4 days done", with a check on each day you've trained this week. A day you missed stays next. Tap the line to open <strong>Which week?</strong>.</li>
    <li>Once every other day of the week is done, Today offers <strong>Skip it and start week 2</strong>. It moves you on the same way as picking the week yourself.</li>
    <li>The section only appears when the routine has a waved day.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3 id="cycle-done">When a cycle ends</h3>
  <p>Log the last day of a cycle and a <strong>Cycle 3 done</strong> sheet opens once, after you tap <strong>Done</strong> on the workout's completion sheet. It appears for programs that run a cycle of weeks off a training max, such as 5/3/1 and nSuns. StrongLifts and GZCLP move through stages instead, so they have no cycle to close.</p>
  <p>The sheet shows, from top to bottom:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>The days you trained, the weeks it ran and the personal records you set.</li>
    <li><strong>Training max</strong> - each lift's number for the next cycle, such as <strong>Squat 140 → 145 kg</strong>. A lift that stays put shows why, for example <em>Holding: the plan's minimum was missed this cycle</em>. These training maxes apply when you start the next cycle.</li>
    <li><strong>Top sets</strong> - each lift's best set, with ▲ when it beat the last cycle's, <strong>Same</strong>, or ▼ when it was lighter.</li>
    <li><strong>Records in this cycle</strong> - the personal records you set, one per exercise.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Three buttons close it. <strong>Keep going</strong> closes the sheet; the next cycle is already set up. <strong>Adjust training maxes</strong> changes the numbers (below). <strong>Change program</strong> opens your routine list. A workout you finish with <strong>End workout</strong> on Today has no completion sheet, so no Cycle done sheet opens for it, but the cycle is still saved under <a href="#past-cycles">Past cycles</a>.</p>

  <h3 id="adjust-training-maxes">Adjust the next cycle's training maxes</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>On the Cycle done sheet, tap <strong>Adjust training maxes</strong>.</li>
    <li>Each lift shows the number the next cycle would use. Change the lifts you want to change.</li>
    <li>Tap <strong>Save</strong>. <strong>Cancel</strong> leaves everything as it was.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>A lift you change uses your number for the whole next cycle, with no increase added on top. It shows in the lift's <a href="#training-max">training max history</a> as your own edit. Lifts you leave alone keep the number the sheet showed.</p>
  <p>The latest cycle's page under <a href="#past-cycles">Past cycles</a> opens the same sheet until the next cycle ends.</p>

  <h3 id="past-cycles">Past cycles</h3>
  <p>Every finished cycle is saved. You find the list in two places:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Cycles</strong>, under the wave on the routine's detail screen, lists that routine's cycles, newest first.</li>
    <li><strong>Looking back</strong>, on <a href="/help/progress/#looking-back">Progress → Overview</a>, lists the cycles of all your programs.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Tap a row to open that cycle's page: the Cycle done sheet as it looked when the cycle ended, without the Next paragraph. Each row keeps the program name it had then, so renaming or deleting a routine leaves its cycles in place.</p>
  <p>Neither list appears until your first cycle finishes. Cycles you finished before this update are not listed, so a program already running counts from cycle 1.</p>

  <h3 id="rotating-exercises">Rotate exercises by week</h3>
  <p>To change which lift you do each week (a board press one week, a good morning the next), add each variation as its own exercise in the day and put all its sets in its week.</p>
  <p>An exercise whose sets all belong to other weeks is left out of that week's preview and workout. An exercise with no planned sets shows up every week.</p>

  <h2 id="stages">Stage ladders</h2>
  <p>A stage ladder moves you to a lighter set scheme when you stall. StrongLifts goes 5×5 → 3×5 → 1×5. GZCLP's T1 goes 5×3+ → 6×2+ → 10×1+, and its T2 goes 3×10 → 3×8 → 3×6.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>The rule's deload is what moves you down: in StrongLifts, three misses in a row drop the weight 10% and move you to the next stage.</li>
    <li>The preview shows <em>Stage 2 of 3</em>. A laddered exercise has no WAVE section, and its stages only change when you stall.</li>
    <li>StrongLifts' deadlift is already 1×5 and has no ladder. If you stall again at 1×5, the program's advice is to move on; Madcow 5×5 is in the Intermediate folder.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="progressing-together">Lifts that progress together</h2>
  <p>When a lift appears on more than one day, those days can share one number and one miss count. In Madcow, Friday's heavy triple sets the weight Monday ramps to. In 5/3/1 BBB, the main lift and its Boring But Big 5×10 are the same lift on the same day, and they read one training max.</p>
  <p>On the preview, the 5×10 line reads <strong>Tap to set the (lift) training max, or tap edit to type a weight</strong>. Tap it to open the sheet for that lift.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>The built-in programs come set up this way. <a href="#set-up-progression">Set up progression</a> can do this for your own routine too, when the same exercise and preset appear on two or more days it's newly writing a rule for; it can't reproduce Madcow's or GZCLP's shape by itself.</li>
    <li>In Madcow, one day moves each lift: Friday for the squat, bench and row, Wednesday for the press and deadlift. The other days take their percentages from that number.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="progressive-overload">Progressive overload suggestion</h2>
  <p>On an exercise without a rule, a <strong>Suggested: 82.5 kg</strong> line appears above the weight field once you've completed all your working sets in each of your last few sessions. <strong>Progressive overload trigger</strong> in <a href="/help/settings/#coaching">Settings → Coaching</a> sets how many (default 3).</p>
  <ul>
    <li>The suggestion is your last session's heaviest weight plus the exercise's increment, rounded to a weight your equipment can make. With a machine set, the machine's step is used.</li>
    <li>It reads history for the machine you have set. Switch machines and the suggestion comes from that machine's history.</li>
    <li>Tap × to dismiss it for this session.</li>
    <li>After eight weeks or more away from an exercise, the sessions before the break don't count. The suggestion comes back once you've done the usual number of sessions since.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3 id="double-progression">Double progression</h3>
  <p>With a <a href="/help/routines/#rep-ranges">rep range</a> on an exercise, you add reps until you reach the top of the range, then add weight and drop back to the bottom.</p>
  <p>While you work up the range, the suggestion reads <strong>Work toward 10 reps</strong>. Once every working set reaches the top for as many sessions as the progressive overload trigger asks, it goes back to suggesting weight.</p>
  <p>An open-topped target (8+) never shows the "work toward" prompt. Exercises with a plain rep target get the weight suggestion only.</p>

  <h3 id="bodyweight-progress">Bodyweight exercises: when to move on</h3>
  <p>Reps Only and Time Only exercises (push-ups, pull-ups, planks) get their own line in the same place. It appears once every working set reaches the target for as many sessions in a row as the progressive overload trigger asks (default 3):</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Reps Only:</strong> 8 reps on every set.</li>
    <li><strong>Time Only:</strong> 30 seconds on every set.</li>
    <li>From a routine with a planned target, the plan wins: the top of a rep range, the exact rep target, or the planned time.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The line names the numbers, for example <strong>8 reps on every set, 3 sessions running. Ready for a harder version.</strong> When the plan sets a different target for different sets, it reads <strong>Every set at its target, 3 sessions running.</strong> A session with a skipped set doesn't count. Under it, <strong>Swap to Decline Push-Up</strong> changes the exercise for today only, the same way <a href="/help/routines/#alternatives">Swap exercise</a> does. Where a weighted version exists, a second link offers it, such as <strong>Swap to Weighted Push-Up</strong>.</p>
  <h4 id="bodyweight-ladders">The harder versions</h4>
  <p>Each movement has a short list, easiest first:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Push: Knee Push-Up, Incline Push-Up, Push-Up, Decline Push-Up, Diamond Push-Up, Archer Push-Up, Weighted Push-Up</li>
    <li>Pull: Assisted Pull-Up, Negative Pull-Up, Pull-Up, Weighted Pull-Up</li>
    <li>Chin: Chin-Up, Weighted Chin-Up</li>
    <li>Dip: Bench Dip, Dips, Weighted Dip</li>
    <li>Vertical push: Pike Push-Up, Handstand Push-Up</li>
    <li>Row: Ring Row or TRX Row, Inverted Row</li>
    <li>Squat: Bodyweight Squat, Split Squat, Bulgarian Split Squat</li>
    <li>Pistol: Assisted Pistol Squat, Single-Leg Squat (Pistol)</li>
    <li>Hip: Glute Bridge, Single-Leg Glute Bridge</li>
    <li>Leg raise: Lying Leg Raise, Hanging Knee Raise, Hanging Leg Raise</li>
    <li>Side hold: Side Plank, Copenhagen Plank</li>
    <li>Front hold: Plank, Hollow Body Hold</li>
  </ul>
  <p>A version you deleted or marked as avoided is skipped. On an exercise at the top of its list, or on no list, the line gives the numbers and says <strong>Add reps or weight.</strong> (for holds, <strong>Hold longer or add weight.</strong>), with no link. An exercise with a progression rule gets no line.</p>

  <h2 id="auto-progress">Auto-progress routine weights</h2>
  <p>Auto-progress offers to write heavier weights you lifted back into the routine, so the next session starts from them. It's off by default: turn on <strong>Auto-progress routine weights</strong> in <a href="/help/settings/#coaching">Settings → Coaching</a>.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Finish a workout you started from a routine.</li>
    <li>On the workout summary, the <strong>Update [Routine] &middot; [Day]?</strong> section lists each exercise you lifted heavier than planned, for example <em>80 → 82.5 kg</em>.</li>
    <li>Uncheck anything you don't want to keep, then tap <strong>Update routine</strong>.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>If <strong>Workout timer auto-stop</strong> is on, the same section also appears on the pop-up when you finish your last set.</p>
  <p>An exercise is offered only when you completed every planned working set, it's the exercise the routine planned (not an alternative you swapped in), and it has no progression rule.</p>
  <p>Weights only go up. Sets are compared one by one, so a pyramid or back-off scheme keeps its shape and only the sets you beat change. Warmups don't count.</p>
</HelpLayout>
